  "summary": "Screened all 52 clients with the update, performance, drift, feature, and subgroup (Sex, AgeGroup, Income) tools, using 19.9 of 20 epsilon. Three clients (CT, MN, MT) show the same strong signature: consistently negative update cosine against the median from round 1 (z between -7 and -8), with normal local AUC and calibration. I reported them as poisoning with moderate confidence. CT also has an unexplained AgeGroup 1.0 subgroup anomaly. Drift, feature-sentinel, and other subgroup flags were small, scattered, or from very small clients (e.g., GU, VI, DE, TN, NC), so I did not report them as faults. Confidence is limited by the low epsilon left for confirmation: a second, independent check of the poisoning hypothesis (for example, a scaled-update test or a narrower update query per round) was not possible."
